 secretion temporarily  increased while testosterone secretion  temporarily  decreased what Jane's fails to mention  was that these levels only change  temporarily and peaked around one hour  after that it just went back to normal  Jane's words it a little bit differently  by saying drinking a cup of cow's milk  can increase the male's Estrin levels by  26% in one hour while dropping their  testosterone levels by 18% so this  suggests that in just an hour it  increases to that much with no mention  of it ever going back down again I'm not  suggesting that these hormonal changes  are a good thing and it's totally your  prerogative to eat whatever you feel  comfortable with but not discussing the  full effect seems a little bit  misleading I do think we need more  context okay so now let's talk about  some of my other grievances with the  film and what I didn't love about it  generally speaking number one the  filmmakers had a clear vegan agenda and  I'm not so sure that that is always so  well cited in a lot of these consumer  entertainment films just like any type  of documentary game-changers had a clear  agenda to get you to follow a vegan diet  it would be the same for you know any  keto or paleo or any other documentary  but the people who make these  documentaries are making them for a  reason and it's their job to make their  case as compelling as possible well I  think that they navigated this pretty  well especially compared to with some  other shocking entries out there I think  it would have been really a nice idea to  emphasize their own personal bias all of  the people who spoke on the film were  either predominantly or completely  plant-based so obviously there's gonna  be a little bit of bias going on now  let's be clear it's impossible to be  completely Switzerland even though you  know I'm trying but we all have our own  unique experiences that really put a  certain bias lens on us however it's  really my opinion that taking the time  to reflect on our biases can help us  make sure that it doesn't affect our  professional opinions that's like that I  have been trying to do here at Abby's  kitchen and I think I would have trusted  the content even more had I seen that  happening if they wanted to appear more  unbiased they really should have  included both sides but instead they  only spoke to people who followed a  plant-based diet or who advocated for a  plant-based diet again it's not as  biases I've seen in other documentary so  I totally applaud that but I still think  it's something that they could have  improved on and still made really really  strong points if not stronger because in  my opinion the science in itself is  compelling enough let the science speak  for itself there were also quite a few  conflicts of interest and biases that  were not mentioned so like I said the  film had doctors experts and athletes  that already follow a plant-based diet  so there is an inherent bias there but  furthermore a lot of the people that  they interviewed had unmentioned  conflicts of interest which I really  don't love so like James Cameron is of  course the filmmaker of movies like  Titanic and Avatar but he's also the  founder and CEO of verdiant foods which  is a vegan pea protein company dr. Aaron  Spitz who's the author of the penis book  which is also a plant-based book dr.  Robert Vogel who's the author of the  Pritikin edge another plant-based book  dr. James Loomis owner of a plant-based  meal planning service dr. Ornish author  of undo it and seller of vegan retreats  and online programs etc etc etc also  when you use celebrities like Arnold  Schwarzenegger to promote a lifestyle  they obviously will have a huge impact  due to their celebrity card so while  these speakers are experienced and  successful in their careers they really  should list their potential conflicts of  interests if they're gonna try to call  out other professionals and researchers  for theirs honestly I understand why  they'd want to interview and incorporate  a wide range of kind of plant-based  scholars and plant-based supporters it  makes total sense I just like the  transparency number three there was  definitely a lack of professional  representation from my industry the  leaders in nutrition so in the film you  see a number of plant-based doctors and  athletes talking about plant-based  eating but that's about it  they don't interview dieticians who are  the experts in nutrition if you're gonna  talk about nutrition  you should probably always have a  dietician at the table  it's like talking about brain health and  not having a neuroscientist or a  neurologist they're the experts  unfortunately there was only one  registered dietician in the field  and it was one that they convene lead  cherry-picked from the CNN interview in  which the dietitian advocates for meat  consumption so it's kind of  disappointing that the only dietitian  that they show is painted as a bit of a  villain when there is a lot a lot a lot  if not pretty much all dietitians that  I'm aware of are really big supporters  of a predominantly plant-based diet  taking a clip from just one dietitian  and generalizing it for all dietitians  is totally unfair and inaccurate so  while the dietitian is correct in saying  that we can't establish a  cause-and-effect relationship between  meat and cancer I think that I can speak  for a lot of dietitians here when I say  that we would all benefit from reducing  our meat consumption and including more  plant-based foods in our diet I think we  personally need to meet people where  they are though and that may mean making  leaner choices or lower sodium choices  or swapping out one meat meal a week  with a plant-based protein etc I've been  pretty clear on my personal belief that  all foods can fit and then it really  does not need to be an all-or-nothing  approach on a side note I recently  watched an interview with James in which  he said that while they probably could  have had more dieticians in the film the  Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics is  sponsored by the meat and dairy industry  okay so even though I'm Canadian and I'm  part of a totally different professional  body I want to make something clear we  are not sponsored or controlled by any  food industry I've spoken about my own  personal involvement in my sponsorship  video here and I agree that's  unfortunate that any food industry has  been sponsoring conferences or events I  don't like that at all but I also know  that learning opportunities like  conferences require money and while I'm  going way off course here I will note  that even at conferences I've been to  where let's say a meat or dairy industry  has signed on as a sponsor I've never  seen an impact the content or learning  that tends to take place and I've been  to lots of great webinars and keynotes  about the benefits of plant-based eating  so for the same reasons I don't like  that doctor's conferences are sponsored  by pharmaceutical companies I get that  it feels icky but I also know that all  of us dietitians are still really  capable of evaluating the research and  making balanced realistic  recommendations for our clients based on  it okay so issue number four  that I just felt there was too much of a  reliance on personal anecdotes  now while personal stories are super  helpful and give examples of the  benefits of the plant-based diet they  aren't really science sure the athletes  say that they feel better and they  probably did but we can't just use their  experience of solid evidence in fact a  lot of their claims regarding sports  performance lack any concrete evidence  or require way more actual real research  before any conclusions can be made  although I'm super happy that these  people found success on a plant-based  diet there are also many arguably even  more successful athletes that follow an  omnivorous diet so highlighting that hey  veganism and athleticism are not  mutually exclusive experience is great  and so important it doesn't negate the  idea that success as an athlete is also  possible following an omnivore diet and  yet because we don't see that experience  or any solid research comparing these we  don't really see that or know that if we  become a too reliant on personal  anecdotes then we're opening up a whole  crazy can of worms where we can make any  claim that we want regarding health and  believe it's true could you imagine if  someone said I started eating two bags  of gummy bears a day and I felt better  and decided that that was enough to  promote a gummy bear diet I know that  it's an extreme example but I see this  stuff happening all the time and it's  like literally why I have a job so on  that note I want to give my overall  conclusion on game-changers I actually  give this film a positive rating I have  tried to be super balanced and critical  here so I hope you can see that while I  fully support plant-based diets it  doesn't mean I'm always fern on  evidence-based ways of promoting them  yes I think their use of some of the  research is faulty  but they do bring up a lot of really  important points and I also think it's  kind of inspiring it definitely helps  dispel the myth that a vegan diet and  athleticism are mutually exclusive I  also think even for us non athletes it  serves a really good reminder that we  would all benefit from a plant-based  diet even if that means just introducing  more plants to our diet in general keep  in mind that you know nutrition science  is extremely complicated  it's not black and white if it was I  mean we'd all be living into our  hundreds trying to jam in nutrition  science into an hour-and-a-half  documentary is nearly impossible so I  commend game-changers for the effort  that they made here and while there are  quite a few flaws in the film I mean  like all films their overall message of  eating more plants is something that I  can totally get on board with so guys I  would love to know if you have watched  game changers what did you like what did  you think of it let me know in the  comments don't forget to subscribe give  this video the thumbs up if you liked my  critical balanced review and I'll see  you next time on Abbey's kitchen bye  [Music]
 

